And I wrote you this little day to send you in New York City.
我为你写了这个小日子，送你在纽约市。

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:23.970
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.809
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈尔德曼，播客《Hyper-Fixed》的主持人。

00:09:27.809 --> 00:09:31.289
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们会收集听众的问题并尝试为他们解决。

00:09:31.289 --> 00:09:34.450
problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的问题：我30岁了，但害怕在纽约开车。

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:38.210
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者为什么我倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.750
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:46.870
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that cause that problem in the first place.
如果你有一个问题，我们不仅会解决它，还会揭示导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.330
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:09:49.330 --> 00:10:07.980
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
你可以在各大播客平台或 hyperfixedpod.com 上找到它。

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.299
I have a question for you though, so just so we know how writer's rooms work.
不过我有个问题想问你，为了让我们了解编剧室是如何运作的。

00:10:12.299 --> 00:10:19.100
So you crack a story, Do you have a beginning, middle, and end by the time you go off to write the first draft?
所以当你构思出一个故事时，在你去写初稿之前，你已经有了开头、经过和结尾吗？

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:48.700
Definitely, that doesn't mean it doesn't change, but we absolutely, you know, Carter and Craig ran a room, and I've tried to do the same when I've been lucky enough to run shows, where let's make sure that this story is as solid as we can possibly make it, and let's beat out every single scene, every, know what the jokes are, know what the A to B of each scene is, And really feel great about it, because as you know, everything gets thrown at you.
肯定的，这并不意味着它不会改变，但我们绝对——你知道的，卡特和克雷格管理着一个编剧室，当我有幸负责制作剧集时我也尝试这样做——确保这个故事尽可能扎实，把每一个场景都细细打磨，清楚笑点是什么，知道每个场景从A到B的发展，并且对此充满信心，因为正如你所知，各种状况都会扑面而来。

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:58.700
Whether it's notes from the Studio Network or suddenly a big set piece that you thought was gonna be hysterical, turns out to not be that hysterical.
无论是来自制片厂或电视台的意见，还是突然间你以为会爆笑的大型布景，结果发现并没有那么搞笑。

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:06.379
So you have to believe in that internal structure, that basic structure of the story as much as possible.
所以你必须尽可能地相信这种内部结构，也就是故事的基本结构。

00:11:06.379 --> 00:11:10.779
Now that doesn't mean that sometimes we do sort of say, oh, you know what, this isn't working the way we thought it did.
这并不是说有时我们不会说：“噢，你知道吗，这个效果和我们想象的不一样。”

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:21.500
But yeah, they were, so, and I'm sure you've heard this before, but like the episode has my name on it, but it's got contributions from every writer in it.
但确实，它们是——而且我确信你以前听过这个，这一集虽然挂着我的名字，但里面凝聚了每一位编剧的贡献。

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:27.059
Every writer, it's been through the room multiple, multiple times by the time, especially by the time we get it to air.
每一位编剧，在剧集最终播出之前，它已经在编剧室里被讨论和修改了无数次。

00:11:27.059 --> 00:11:30.220
This is probably a question you'll be uncomfortable in answering, but I want you to try.
这可能是一个让你回答起来不太舒服的问题，但我想让你试一试。

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:42.890
Are there moments from that draft that made it into the show, that made it onto the air, that are Chris Harris, that are pure Chris Harris, You're like I'm excited and happy about this so many.
初稿中有哪些片段最终被保留并播出了，那是属于克里斯·哈里斯的、纯粹属于克里斯·哈里斯的特色，让你觉得既兴奋又高兴？

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:52.213
I would say I don't want Chris to be shy so many Chris has such a high hit rate of that in every script he wrote Your praises there's so much.
有很多。我可不想让克里斯害羞，克里斯在每个他写的剧本里都有着极高的命中率。你的赞誉在那里，有太多了。

00:11:52.213 --> 00:11:55.299
There's so much that was that was other people.
有太多其实是别人的功劳。我可以说这话，看他这样做。看他把功劳都推掉。

00:11:55.299 --> 00:11:59.107
I can say that watch him do this Watch him take no credit.
我知道，我经常看到马修非常擅长游戏，而那个连环包袱——

00:11:59.107 --> 00:12:12.960
I know I see all the time the marshal being really good at games and and the and the what runner I am The one runner, I'm pretty sure, was me sitting alone in a cafe or a restaurant somewhere giggling to myself.
那个连环包袱，我确信是我某天独自坐在咖啡馆或餐馆里自己在那儿傻笑想出来的。

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.960
The one runner is funnier every time.
那个连环包袱一次比一次好笑。

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.960
The one runner is so funny.
那个连环包袱太搞笑了。

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:18.960
It's like a staircase of laughter.
就像是一层层叠加的笑声。

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.960
I want to say that it's such a Chris Harris joke.
我想说这真是一个典型的克里斯·哈里斯式的笑点。

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:35.120
Chris Harris is a master at creating these Rube Goldberg machines, sort of joke delivery systems that are like, like being picked up like and you know that this thing is gonna make this sound at this time and this thing can come back around at this time.
克里斯·哈里斯是创作这种鲁伯·戈德堡机械式笑料传递系统的专家，那种设计就像是被精心拾起，你知道这个东西会在这个时候发出这种声音，而那个东西又会在那个时候呼应出现。

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:36.720
And what's the one Marshall says?
马修说的那句是什么来着？

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.720
He doesn't say why he says when is that right?
他没说为什么，他说“什么时候对过？”

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.919
Robyn says which I had not did not remember.
罗宾说的那句话我都记不得了。

00:12:41.919 --> 00:12:43.720
That made me laugh the hardest of anything.
那让我笑得最大声。

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.120
That was funny.
那个太好笑了。

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:45.960
That was my two.
那是我的两个之一。

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:47.759
I totally came out of the blue.
我完全没想到。

00:12:47.759 --> 00:13:00.360
I mean, watching the whole episode, it kind of, there's a little part of me that made me depressed because I like to think that my writing is getting better and better And over the years, I've just improved.
我是说，看完整集后，我心里有点沮丧，因为我总觉得我的写作水平在不断提高，这些年我一直在进步。

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.679
But I looked, I watched the episode, I was like, ah, damn it, you guys are dead.
但当我看了那一集后，我就想，啊，该死，你们太厉害了。

00:13:05.679 --> 00:13:08.639
It was my year, 2006, it was my year.
那是我的年份，2006年，那是我的年份。

00:13:08.639 --> 00:13:11.720
It wasn't my year, it was so far over the hill.
那才不是我的年份呢，早就走下坡路了。

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.639
It's not often that you can point to the moment you peaked, but this is it.
你很少能准确指出自己达到巅峰的时刻，但这就是。

00:13:15.639 --> 00:13:20.320
That was it, it's just been a very, thankfully, a very slow decline, but a decline nonetheless.
就是那样，谢天谢地，这只是一个非常缓慢的衰退，但确实在衰退。

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.799
Why did we keep around eight more years after that?
为什么在那之后我们还坚持了八年？

00:13:22.799 --> 00:13:24.360
And slow decline, never.
缓慢衰退，才没有。

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.360
You kept pitching that watch joke over and over again.
你一直在反复抛出那个关于手表的笑话。

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:28.480
No, it's a callback and people will love it.
不，这是一个呼应，大家会喜欢的。

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:38.159
You know, I love not only not only is Marshall great at games, my first big laugh when he screams Yahtzee, like that's a pure seagull, just like that's a pure seagull.
你知道的，我不仅喜欢……不仅马歇尔游戏玩得好，当他尖叫喊出“Yahtzee”时，我第一次放声大笑，听起来就像一只纯种海鸥，真的就像一只纯种海鸥。

00:13:38.159 --> 00:13:39.399
They probably didn't write it that way.
他们可能不是那样写的。

00:13:39.399 --> 00:13:43.759
It's just like a weird take and such a fun word to scream.
这只是一种奇怪的演绎，而且这个词喊出来太有意思了。

00:13:43.759 --> 00:13:48.399
But where did you guys come up with Marshall's so good at games, he's invented his own?
但你们是怎么想到“马歇尔游戏玩得太好了，以至于自己发明了新游戏”这个创意的？

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:51.759
Which crazily enough, I never even thought of this.
说来也疯狂，我以前甚至没想过这个。

00:13:51.759 --> 00:13:54.720
Did that freak Lily out, given what her thought, You guys didn't know that.
考虑到莉莉的想法，这有没有吓到她？你们当时并不知道这一点。

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.240
We didn't know that, but then we later arrived at Chris.
我们当时不知道，但后来我们想到了克里斯。

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:58.960
Chasing board game fortunes.
追逐棋盘游戏的财富。

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:04.840
Yeah, we later arrived at the idea that Chris Elliott as her dad would have been this board game inventor and realized there was this kind of overlap.
对，我们后来才想到克里斯·埃利奥特扮演的她父亲会是一个棋盘游戏发明家，然后意识到这里面有一种重合。

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:06.279
Like she did marry her dad.
就像她嫁给了自己的父亲一样。

00:14:06.279 --> 00:14:07.679
She did marry her dad.
她确实嫁给了自己的父亲。

00:14:07.679 --> 00:14:10.440
She was trying to like move on to life and work for the game.
她试图开启新生活并为游戏工作。

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.559
We realized that very retroactively though.
不过我们是很马后炮地才意识到这一点的。

00:14:12.559 --> 00:14:14.480
I'm not even, I'm not sure we did that on purpose at all.
我甚至不确定我们是不是故意这样做的。

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:15.320
I was just like, yeah.
我当时就想，好吧。

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:19.159
But that's another one of those like, those unconscious writer connections that you're making.
但这也是那种编剧无意识中建立的联系之一。

00:14:19.159 --> 00:14:20.000
Unconscious connections.
无意识的联系。

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:20.840
Yeah.
对。

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:21.799
That's hysterical.
太滑稽了。

00:14:21.799 --> 00:14:22.639
I never thought about that.
我从来没想过这个。

00:14:22.639 --> 00:14:23.480
Wow.
哇。

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:27.377
I thought it was particularly funny that Marshall is so good at games.
我觉得特别搞笑的是马歇尔游戏玩得太好，

00:14:27.377 --> 00:14:30.440
He has to invent his own Like where did that come with?
以至于他不得不发明自己的游戏。这灵感是从哪来的？

00:14:31.159 --> 00:14:53.399
I remember it originally it was it was March Opoli or Marshall Opoli Oh, yeah, we could not get the rights cleared to Marshall Opoli and I remember we were actually on set and we had like we were allowed to use Candyland visually But something about the Opoli was just throwing legal for a loop Wow.
我记得最初它是“Marsh-opoly”或“Marshall-opoly”。哦，对，我们没能搞定“Marshall-opoly”的版权，我记得当时我们正在片场，视觉上我们可以使用“坎迪王国”（Candyland），但“opoly”这个词让法务部门伤透了脑筋。哇。

00:14:53.399 --> 00:15:00.039
So you had to go back to a game that's been around for thousands of years, the Mesopotamians didn't copyright that gamut.
所以你们不得不回到一个存在了数千年的游戏，美索不达米亚人可没有给那个游戏申请版权。

00:15:00.039 --> 00:15:01.039
No.
没有。

00:15:01.039 --> 00:15:08.159
Who would have thought the Monopoly people would play a real corporate hardball?
谁能想到大富翁（Monopoly）的人会玩真的企业强硬手段呢？

00:15:08.159 --> 00:15:14.080
You should have paid them in Monopoly, Mark, to see if they were taken in.
你应该用大富翁的钱付给他们，马克，看看他们收不收。

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.360
That's why there are so few games that end with Opelie.
这就是为什么以opoly结尾的游戏那么少。

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.279
Rebecca had a question, Chris.
丽贝卡有个问题，克里斯。

00:15:18.279 --> 00:15:21.639
She said, did Chris Harris really hit backgammon that much?
她说，克里斯·哈里斯真的那么讨厌西洋双陆棋吗？

00:15:21.639 --> 00:15:24.796
Marshall's like weirdly rage-filled dig at backgammon?
马歇尔对西洋双陆棋那种莫名其妙充满愤怒的挖苦？

00:15:24.796 --> 00:15:27.836
I feel like maybe you did hate backgammon that much.
我觉得也许你当时确实那么讨厌西洋双陆棋。

00:15:27.836 --> 00:15:28.480
Am I right?
我说的对吗？

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:29.438
I don't think so.
我不这么觉得。

00:15:29.438 --> 00:15:34.679
In fact, I looked, well, one, I think that if I had to pick, I think that's your joke, Craig.
事实上，我查过，呃，第一，我觉得如果非要选的话，我觉得那是你的玩笑，克雷格。

00:15:34.679 --> 00:15:35.731
I could be wrong.
我也可能记错了。

00:15:35.731 --> 00:15:36.231
Really?
真的吗？

00:15:36.231 --> 00:15:36.731
Sure.
当然。

00:15:36.731 --> 00:15:37.411
I think so.
我觉得是。

00:15:37.411 --> 00:15:43.347
And then two, in the first draft, he was just as angry about monopoly, which might have been me.
然后第二，在初稿里，他对大富翁游戏的痛恨程度不相上下，那可能是我写的。

00:15:43.347 --> 00:15:49.097
I might have hated monopoly enough, but then we just moved that hatred over to a different...
我可能对大富翁恨之入骨，但后来我们把这种痛恨转移到了另一个……

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:53.600
Oh my god, if that unending to me, I literally to this day, I don't really know what that gammon is.
天哪，如果那个游戏对我来说没完没了的话，老实说直到今天，我其实都不知道那是个什么游戏。

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.840
So I can't say I have hold any video against something I don't understand.
所以我不能说我对一个不了解的东西有什么怨言。

00:15:59.759 --> 00:16:03.279
But I think the being really good at games came from you a little Chris, didn't it?
但我觉得非常擅长玩游戏这一点，多多少少是受了你的影响吧，克里斯，对吗？

00:16:03.279 --> 00:16:07.840
Because just to share this and we can edit this out if you want, you're a bit of a, you're a game guy.
因为顺便说一句——如果你愿意的话我们可以把这段剪掉——你其实挺那个的，你是个游戏迷。

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:09.480
You're a scavenger hunt guy.
你是个寻宝游戏迷。

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:15.879
You are an adult, elevated, travel to another city you don't live in, scavenger hunt guy, true or false.
你是一个成年人、高级玩家、会跑到自己不住的另一个城市去玩寻宝游戏的人，对还是错？

00:16:15.879 --> 00:16:18.440
Why would you, why would you want to edit this out?
你为什么，你为什么想把这段剪掉？

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:23.440
Why do you think this is interesting to people about how much I like puzzles and games?
你为什么觉得大家会对我有多少喜欢解谜和游戏感兴趣？

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:24.440
You know, I do.
你知道的，我是。

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:25.440
This is you, right?
这就是你，对吧？

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.440
This is you, right?
这就是你，对吧？

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:27.440
I love puzzles.
我超爱解谜。

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.440
I did.
我确实喜欢。

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:33.440
Yeah, I used to do these hunts where you drive around for 30 hours in a van with a team going from clue to clue, solving other puzzles.
对，我以前经常参加那种寻宝活动，和团队一起开着面包车在里面转30个小时，从一个线索到另一个线索，解开各种谜题。

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:34.440
30 hours.
30个小时。

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:35.440
It's crazy.
太疯狂了。

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:37.440
And Neil has a little bit of that, too.
尼尔也有点这方面的爱好。

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.440
We had a little bit of overlap with that, too.
我们在这方面也有过一点交集。

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.440
You know, it's true.
你知道，确实如此。

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:44.440
I mean, a lot of writers, like Steven Sondheim very famously, was a huge crossword puzzle guy, right?
我的意思是，很多编剧，比如斯蒂芬·桑德海姆就非常出名，是个超级填字游戏迷，对吧？

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:45.440
Yeah.
对。

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:52.399
And I think there's something about the brain, the comedy brain, lyricist brain that when things land in the right place, it's so satisfying, right?
而且我觉得人的大脑里，有某种喜剧大脑、作词家大脑的特质，当一切各就各位时，那种感觉太满足了，对吧？

00:16:52.399 --> 00:16:54.052
There's a little bit of a puzzle to it.
这本身就带有一点解谜的性质。

00:16:54.052 --> 00:16:54.552
Yeah.
对。

00:16:54.552 --> 00:16:58.196
And, and, and thinking, thinking, yeah, certainly when you're did all sorts of things.
而且，而且，而且思考，思考，是的，当你做过各种各样的事情的时候。

00:16:58.196 --> 00:16:58.696
Yeah.
对。

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:05.789
When you're 15 episodes into a 22 episode season, you, it is a puzzle because you have this dictate, like, okay, we have to get the plot here.
当你在一部22集的季里拍到第15集时，你，这确实是个谜题，因为你有这样的硬性要求，比如，好吧，我们必须把剧情推进到这里。

00:17:05.789 --> 00:17:11.244
We have to move the plot here, but we also, we have to have it funny and it has to be self-contained.
我们必须把剧情推进到这里，但同时，我们得让它搞笑，而且还得是独立的。

00:17:11.244 --> 00:17:22.099
If you're just tuning in for this one episode, we want audiences to kind of follow it, but you also to want to reward people who've been watching from episode one, like there is a lot of puzzle making.
如果你只是碰巧收看这一集，我们希望观众能够顺着看懂，但你也希望奖励那些从第一集就开始看的观众，所以这里面有大量的解谜工作。

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:23.803
And that's a huge challenge.
这真是一个巨大的挑战。

