你说得对，你知道我的意思。

00:41:07.219 --> 00:41:14.039
I also, you know, speaking about Barney, you think he's gonna transform it.
我也，你知道的，说到巴尼，你以为他会转变。

00:41:14.039 --> 00:41:19.239
We've talked about this, Craig, but I remember this great editor I worked with on both my movies, Michael Miller.
我们谈过这个，克莱格，但我记得和我拍两部电影时合作过的一位伟大的剪辑师，迈克尔·米勒。

00:41:19.239 --> 00:41:22.880
He talked about the difference between film and TV or one of the main differences.
他谈到过电影和电视的区别，或者说主要的区别之一。

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:30.239
On TV shows, a transformation has to be much slower because you're trying to establish these characters and they have their groove, they have their lane.
在电视剧中，转变必须慢得多，因为你在努力确立这些角色的形象，他们有自己的节奏，有自己的轨道。

00:41:30.239 --> 00:41:35.340
And you slowly, slowly transform them, but you can't transform them too quickly, or the show is over.
然后你慢慢地、慢慢地改变他们，但你不能改变得太快，否则剧就结束了。

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:41.739
Whereas in a movie, you've got to transform them in 90 minutes or two hours, like transformation happened to much speedier.
而在电影里，你必须在90分钟或两小时内改变他们，就像转变发生得要快得多。

00:41:41.739 --> 00:41:48.019
And I, you know, thinking about, you plant the seeds for, okay, there's a different guy underneath this suit.
而且我，你知道，想着，你为之埋下伏笔，好吧，这套西装底下其实是另一个人。

00:41:48.019 --> 00:41:55.820
The suit is armor, the suit is a character, he's playing at being a villain on some level, but it's a, it's a, it's a character he's not done playing.
西装是铠甲，西装是一个角色，他在某种程度上扮演着恶棍，但这是一个他还没演够的角色。

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:56.820
He's going to keep playing it.
他会继续演下去。

00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:59.219
So tune in next week, he's still going to be this guy.
所以请收看下周的节目，他还是会是这个家伙。

00:41:59.219 --> 00:42:00.219
Yeah.
对。

00:42:00.219 --> 00:42:05.219
And eventually over time, he's got, we saw more and more, we really got back to Barney's childhood.
最终随着时间的推移，他不得不，我们看到了越来越多，我们真正回到了巴尼的童年。

00:42:05.219 --> 00:42:16.500
And we saw, we really saw where that guy began, where all the wounds went and sort of connected that through land, but the gift of long-form TV is getting to discover that, getting to peel back those layers.
我们看到了，我们真正看到了那个家伙是从哪里开始的，所有的创伤都去了哪里，并把这些脉络连了起来，但长篇电视剧的天赋就在于能够去发现这一切，去剥开那些层层外壳。

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:17.860
We didn't know all of that.
我们并不是一开始就知道这一切的。

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:20.539
We didn't know Barney was a hippie until we said that, it'd be funny.
在我们说出来之前，我们甚至不知道巴尼曾经是个嬉皮士，不过这挺逗的。

00:42:20.539 --> 00:42:21.380
It'd be funny.
挺逗的。

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:23.059
It'd be funny to have him, isn't that weird?
让他这样不是很奇怪吗？

00:42:23.059 --> 00:42:23.900
It was like a dorm scene.
这就像一个宿舍的场景。

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:32.619
The next eight years, like you then, almost like the vomit free, like you have to contend with the things you lay down because they have a cascade of consequences from that.
接下来的八年里，就像，就像免吐环节一样，你必须应对你曾经铺垫过的东西，因为它们会带来一连串的后果。

00:42:32.619 --> 00:42:35.980
Paint yourself into a corner and that's, that will even get out of it.
把自己逼进死胡同，然后，那总能脱身的。

00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:36.820
Watch how to get out of it.
看怎么脱身吧。

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:40.380
The necessity is the mother of invention, like you have to make yourself solve that problem.
需求是发明之母，你必须逼自己去解决那个问题。

00:42:41.579 --> 00:42:50.539
Yeah, and Chris, I feel like you, in, okay, awesome, I remember the moment where we realized the narrator could have forgotten to tell you a crucial detail.
是的，克里斯，我觉得你，在，哦太棒了，我记得那个时刻：我们意识到叙述者可能忘记告诉你一个至关重要的细节。

00:42:50.539 --> 00:42:51.380
Oh, yes.
哦，是的。

00:42:51.380 --> 00:42:52.739
I remember the idea of this.
我记得这个点子。

00:42:52.739 --> 00:42:56.420
Oh, by the way, you had this dental procedure earlier in the day and you freeze frame.
哦，顺便说一句，你当天早些时候做过这个牙科手术，然后画面定格。

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:58.619
And like, we are still finding it.
而且，我们还在摸索中。

00:42:58.619 --> 00:43:07.500
We're finding the moves and this episode is the template for so many of those future round table flashback episodes where we learned you need a present day conundrum.
我们在寻找套路，这一集成为了后来许多圆桌会议式闪回剧集的模板，我们从中了解到你需要一个当下的难题。

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:08.500
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:10.940
The flashbacks need to have twists and turns of their own.
闪回本身也需要有起伏转折。

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:13.820
You need each flashback to be different one from the other.
你需要让每一次闪回都各不相同。

00:43:13.820 --> 00:43:18.219
You need to be really moving the story forward in the present or else it's just like a clip show.
你需要让故事在当下真正推进，否则它就只是个精彩片段集锦。

00:43:18.219 --> 00:43:19.900
Like this was us figuring out how to do that kind of episode.
就像这是我们在摸索如何制作那种类型的剧集。

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:22.059
We did a whole bunch more kind of like this one.
我们还拍了好多类似这样的剧集。

00:43:22.059 --> 00:43:26.900
And it was so fun and you're right, we were sort of clawing away and sometimes feeling our way in the dark.
这太有趣了，你说得对，我们当时有点像在摸索前行，有时是在黑暗中凭感觉摸索。

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:39.920
at some point in the room like just us realizing, oh, the flashback can't just show what we just said it was going to show, which, like, you know, I've seen that pitfall in other places.
在编剧室的某个时刻，我们突然意识到，哦，闪回不能只是展示我们刚刚说过它要展示的内容，你知道的，我在其他地方见过这种陷阱。

00:43:39.920 --> 00:43:50.699
And yeah, I think you're right, that present-day story is the most important thing because that's what's carrying you through the great story in the past.
是的，我想你是对的，当下的故事才是最最重要的，因为那是支撑你穿梭于过去那些伟大故事的纽带。

00:43:50.699 --> 00:43:57.760
In regards to this talk of Barney and his origin story, Josh, would you like to introduce Chris to this our very important section.
关于巴尼和他的身世背景这个话题，乔什，你想向克里斯介绍我们的这个非常重要的环节吗？

00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:11.260
Yeah, this is a now beloved section of the show that we like to call questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
好的，这是节目中现在备受喜爱的环节，我们喜欢称之为：来自一位从未看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》且恰好是乔什妻子的临床心理学家的疑问与观察。

00:44:11.260 --> 00:44:17.780
This episode, to me, is really like the Barney origin story, and I think it's great.
对我来说，这一集真的很像巴尼的起源故事，我觉得它太棒了。

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:24.598
It gave me so much more of an understanding of him, explanation for his behavior, for is bravado.
它让我对他的理解加深了许多，也为他的行为和虚张声势提供了解释。

00:44:24.598 --> 00:44:26.074
It's like, oh, right.
就像是，哦，原来如此。

00:44:26.074 --> 00:44:28.393
It all comes from a broken heart.
这一切都源于一颗破碎的心。

00:44:28.393 --> 00:44:29.659
Of course it does.
当然是的。

00:44:29.659 --> 00:44:42.171
His insensitivity, quote-unquote, you know, is actually a way to protect himself from how deeply sensitive he is and how wounded he was from making himself incredibly vulnerable.
他所谓的“麻木不仁”，你知道的，实际上是一种保护自己的方式，为了掩饰他是多么的敏感，以及当他让自己变得极度脆弱时受过多么严重的伤害。

00:44:42.171 --> 00:45:01.219
So I don't have that much to say other than that, it's a good reminder that this is almost always the case that the the people who present as tough and invulnerable were often once very sensitive, but that part of them has been hurt and has subsequently gone underground.
所以除了这些我没有太多要说的了，这是一个很好的提醒：这种情况几乎总是存在的，那些表现得坚强且刀枪不入的人，往往曾经都非常敏感，但他们那一部分受到了伤害，随后便被潜藏了起来。

00:45:01.219 --> 00:45:06.260
Not a lot of talk about how handsome the writer of the episode is, but you know, it's like other than that.
虽然没怎么夸这集的编剧有多帅，但你知道，除此之外就是这样。

00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:08.099
We had another one and I killed that.
我们还有另一个点子，我把它毙了。

00:45:08.099 --> 00:45:09.139
I killed that one.
那个点子被我毙了。

00:45:09.139 --> 00:45:10.500
I was like, that's an approach.
我当时说，那是一种处理方式。

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:12.019
I don't want you doing that one.
我不想让你用那个。

00:45:12.019 --> 00:45:13.099
Do one about Barney.
换个关于巴尼的。

00:45:14.260 --> 00:45:15.420
No, it's a great observation.
不，这是一个很棒的观察。

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:16.420
It's totally.
完全是这样。

00:45:16.420 --> 00:45:17.860
You need to see that side of Barney.
你需要看到巴尼的那一面。

00:45:17.860 --> 00:45:20.739
You need to see that side of Barney or he's not interesting anymore.
你必须看到巴尼的那一面，否则他就不有趣了。

00:45:20.739 --> 00:45:24.820
He's only interesting, I think, because we get to see where it comes from.
我认为他之所以有趣，仅仅是因为我们得以了解这一切的根源。

00:45:24.820 --> 00:45:27.059
I think that's what makes him so much more human.
我认为这正是让他变得更有血有肉的原因。

00:45:27.059 --> 00:45:30.539
It's also counter-intuitive culturally, especially for men.
这在文化上也与直觉相反，尤其是对男性而言。

00:45:30.539 --> 00:45:35.300
Like, I love the phrase rudeness is the weak man's attempt at strength.
比如，我很喜欢这句话：“粗鲁是弱者对力量的模仿”。

00:45:35.300 --> 00:45:51.860
You know, there's something about, especially in the moment we're in where there's so much confusion about masculinity and what is healthy masculinity, and there's these forward facing louder men in the world that are much more actually bro-cody.
你知道，这其中有些深意，特别是在我们当前这个时代，对男子气概以及什么是健康的男子气概有着如此多的混淆，世界上总有一些表现得好胜且更大声的男性，他们其实更加大男子主义。

00:45:51.860 --> 00:46:05.860
You know, they're much more kind of, and we've gotten in the weeds on some of this, but I'm sure we'll unpack it more just like, you know, you guys were not holding up Barney as any kind of exemplar of like male behavior.
你知道的，他们更加那种，而且我们已经在其中一些问题上纠缠不清了，但我确信我们会进一步剖析它，就像，你知道的，你们并没有把巴尼奉为什么男性行为的楷模。

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:12.420
Like you were, you know, you guys are all, it was a writer's room of Ted's and Marshall's, you know, the man, right?
就像你们，你知道的，你们所有人，那是一个由泰德和马歇尔组成的编剧室，懂吧？

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:22.380
Like it wasn't, I think it's just useful to remember that vulnerability, especially publicly is quite difficult and requires an enormous amount of strength.
就像它不是，我认为记住这一点很有用：脆弱，尤其是公开表现出脆弱，是非常困难的，需要极其巨大的力量。

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:27.380
And I think being kind of shut down and rude doesn't.
而我认为那种封闭自己和粗鲁的行为则不需要。

00:46:27.900 --> 00:46:38.039
So I don't know, I still, I love and appreciate Barney as a character, but I think you have to watch him with the right understanding.
所以，我不知道，我依然热爱并欣赏巴尼这个角色，但我认为你必须带着正确的理解来看待他。

00:46:38.039 --> 00:46:38.880
Yeah.
对。

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:39.719
That's interesting.
这很有意思。

00:46:39.719 --> 00:46:48.949
There is that danger of like the thing that we're mocking is the thing that people love about the character, genuinely love instead of loving to laugh at.
确实存在那种危险，即我们原本在嘲讽的东西，反而成了人们真心喜爱这个角色的原因，是真正的喜爱，而不是觉得好笑。

00:46:48.949 --> 00:46:59.670
I think season one, we actually did a very good job of showing like, oh, Barney, to this point in season one, he has not succeeded in hugging up with that many women.
我认为在第一季里，我们其实做得非常好，展示了诸如：哦，巴尼，直到第一季的这个阶段，他在泡妞这件事上并没有取得多大成功。

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:02.849
He's been very concerned about whether Ted's his best friend or not.
他一直很在意泰德是不是他最好的朋友。

00:47:02.849 --> 00:47:05.829
And we learned that he used to be a hippie who had his heart broken.
而我们了解到，他曾经是个心碎过的嬉皮士。

00:47:05.829 --> 00:47:13.429
Like, I really like how in season one, we did keep our eye on the ball, I think, of like, this guy, there's a lot underneath that suit.
就像，我真的很喜欢我们在第一季中一直专注于一点，我是说，这个家伙，那套西装下面有很多内容。

00:47:13.429 --> 00:47:15.171
this episode is basically that.
这集基本上就是讲这个。

00:47:15.171 --> 00:47:18.710
That's the core of this episode is what's underneath that suit.
这一集的核心就是那套西装下面藏着什么。

00:47:18.710 --> 00:47:30.686
And I think in later seasons, sometimes we lost sight of that for different patches of time and started to lose the kind of ironic distance from it and sometimes did the thing rather than were ironically commenting on the thing.
而且我认为在后来的几季中，有时我们在不同时期会忽略这一点，开始失去那种反讽的距离感，有时是直接去表现那件事，而不是带着反讽的眼光去评论它。

00:47:30.686 --> 00:47:34.521
What I like about this episode is we're saying, this is the premise here.
我喜欢这一集的地方在于，我们在表达：这就是前提。

00:47:34.521 --> 00:47:52.591
The premise here is there is someone who is inside of a Darth Vader costume and there's more to him than meets the eye and that's so much more interesting than a guy who like just hooks up with lots of women and like seems really cool like I want to see what who is that guy really and that's that's what's that's the best part of this episode.
前提是，有一个人穿着达斯·维达的服装，他身上有比外表更多的内涵，这比一个只是跟很多女人鬼混、看起来很酷的家伙要有趣得多，我想看看这人到底是谁，这就是这一集最棒的部分。

00:47:52.591 --> 00:48:12.762
So I'm just curious just from this vantage point you know it's been 11 years since the show ended it's been 20 years since the show premiered so I'm just curious like when you look back on that time like what are your biggest memories what do you what do you feel when you see how I met your mother in the world, when you hear about all these younger generations discovering the show.
所以我只是好奇，从这个角度来看，你知道，距离这部剧完结已经过去11年了，距离首播已经20年了，所以我只是好奇，当你回首那个时代时，你最大的记忆是什么，当你看到《老爸老妈浪漫史》在这个世界上产生影响，当你听到所有这些年轻一代在发现这部剧时，你有什么感觉？

00:48:12.762 --> 00:48:16.439
I mean, you were at the absolute epicenter of this for the entire run.
我的意思是，整个播放期间，你一直处於这个事件绝对的核心。

00:48:16.439 --> 00:48:19.381
So like, where does how I met your mother live with you?
所以，《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你来说意味着什么？

00:48:19.381 --> 00:48:21.009
What are your thoughts on that?
对此你有什么想法？

00:48:21.010 --> 00:48:37.863
I think one thing that we wanted was having been on plenty of shows before and plenty of shows after, if you get a great show, great people around you or great hours, if you get one of those on a show, then you're lucky.
我想我们想要的一点是，经历过很多次播前和播后的节目之后，如果你能遇到一个好节目，身边有一群好人，或者工作时间很好，如果你能在节目中得到其中之一，那你就算很幸运了。

00:48:37.863 --> 00:48:46.673
One thing we were good at was being aware in the moment of how fortunate we all were to be working with each other.
我们擅长的一点是，在当时就意识到我们能彼此共事是多么幸运。

00:48:46.673 --> 00:48:57.551
I give Cardin Craig all the credit in the world, along with Pam and Susie for fostering that, appreciating where we are and what we get to do.
我要把所有的赞誉归功于卡丁和克雷格，还有帕姆和苏西，感谢他们营造了这种氛围，让我们懂得珍惜我们所处的位置和我们所做的事。

00:48:57.551 --> 00:49:08.430
Honestly, I've carried, not to sound too mushy, but I've I've carried that with me for these past decades, and I'm incredibly grateful for it.
说实话，我一直把这些——听起来可能有点多愁善感——但我把这些带进了过去的几十年里，对此我感激不尽。

00:49:08.429 --> 00:49:16.989
I was at a talking at a school a few days ago, and this teacher in his probably mid-20s just came up and said, I can't believe you wrote for how much.
几天前我在一所学校做演讲，这个大概二十五岁左右的老师走过来对我说，我真不敢相信你给这个节目写过剧本。

00:49:16.989 --> 00:49:21.909
Like, you know, still excited.
就像，你知道的，依然很激动。

00:49:21.909 --> 00:49:34.530
Another friend, his 12-year-old daughter just finished the run of the series, which I mixed feelings about knowing some of the things that we wrote, but I still love it.
另一个朋友，他12岁的女儿刚刚追完整部剧，虽然我知道我们写的一些内容后对这事有些复杂的情感，但我还是很喜欢。

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:49.369
And I also, there's one, I don't know why this is the one moment I remember, but hearing the Josh who gave us that intro, his story about addiction, everything.
而且我也，不知为什么这是我记住的唯一一个时刻，听到给我们做那个开场白乔希讲述他关于戒瘾的故事，以及种种经历。

00:49:50.409 --> 00:50:03.570
I always remember this one moment midway through the series being at some event or something, and the rest of the event I don't remember, but all I remember is this one woman saying, Thanks for making my Mondays a little happier.
我总是记得剧集播到中间时的某个时刻，在某个活动上，活动的其他部分我都不记得了，我只记得有个女人说：“谢谢你们让我的周一过得更开心一点。”

00:50:03.570 --> 00:50:22.670
And, you know, I think that's wonderful that we got to do something that we all believed in and all loved and got to put out there and hopefully made brief little moments of different people's lives a little happier.
而且，你知道的，我觉得这太棒了，我们做了一件我们都相信并且都热爱的事，把它呈现出来，希望能让不同的人在短暂的日常生活中感到一丝快乐。

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:34.989
So yeah, I think we knew in the moment, especially as that last season started to happen, like this might, we might never have a job as this.
所以是的，我想当时我们心里有数，特别是当最后一季开始播出时，这可能会——我们可能再也不会有一份这样的工作了。

00:50:34.989 --> 00:50:35.989
It might, yes.
它可能会，是的。

00:50:35.989 --> 00:50:39.550
And that's okay, because we got to have this job.
但这没关系，因为我们曾经拥有过这份工作。

00:50:39.550 --> 00:50:41.510
You know, we got to have these amazing nine years.
你知道，我们拥有了这神奇的九年。

00:50:41.510 --> 00:50:52.550
So much as I would love to do another show that I care about as deeply as that, I'm really happy that I got to do this one.
尽管我很想再做一部我像这样深切关心的剧，但我真的很庆幸我做过这一部。

00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:54.710
Craig, maybe let's interrogate this.
克雷格，也许我们来探讨一下这个。

00:50:54.710 --> 00:50:58.230
It's not an issue now because people are watching this whatever they want.
现在这不是问题了，因为人们随时随地都在看。

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.789
But it just occurred to me and said, thank you for making my Mondays better.
但它刚才突然出现在我脑海里，（有人）说谢谢你们让我的周一变得更好。

00:51:01.789 --> 00:51:03.590
Mondays are hard days.
周一是艰难的日子。

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:05.030
Mondays the start of the week.
周一是新一周的开始。

00:51:05.030 --> 00:51:07.750
Mondays like you got the whole week ahead of you.
周一就像整个星期都在你前方等着你。

00:51:07.750 --> 00:51:10.110
And that we were on Monday nights.
而我们是在周一晚上播出。

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:12.829
I think that's like a little bit part of the story in a weird way.
我觉得这在某种奇怪的层面上也是故事的一部分。

00:51:12.829 --> 00:51:13.670
I think so too.
我也这么认为。

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:14.750
I think we were on Monday night.
我觉得我们是在周一晚上播出。

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:17.269
You had to slog your way through Monday.
你必须咬牙熬过周一。

00:51:17.269 --> 00:51:19.909
And if you watched Monday night, there we were for you.
如果你在周一晚上观看，我们就陪伴着你。

00:51:19.909 --> 00:51:20.789
And it was once a week.
而且是一周一次。

00:51:20.789 --> 00:51:22.590
And that's how people consume the show.
大家就是这样看节目的。

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:27.269
And young people have no idea quite the felt like to consume the show in that way.
而现在的年轻人完全无法想象以这种方式追剧是什么感觉。

00:51:27.269 --> 00:51:37.670
Though I will say that we have gotten not just one response online thanking people again for their Mondays, saying just like on the shows this week, this podcast comes out on Monday.
不过我要说的是，我们在网上收到的不只是一个回应，有人再次感谢大家带给他们美好的周一，他们说就像这周的节目一样，这个播客是在周一发布的。

00:51:37.670 --> 00:51:41.030
It's just like we wait for the show on Monday, we wait for the podcast.
就像我们在周一等待节目，我们在周一等待播客。

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:48.309
I just want to say Chris Harris is the dream writer to have on your writing staff and has been an amazing showrunner of other stuff now too.
我只想说，克里斯·哈里斯是你编剧团队中的理想编剧，现在他也是其他节目的出色运作人了。

00:51:48.309 --> 00:51:50.150
And like, Chris, thank you.
就像，克里斯，谢谢你。

00:51:50.150 --> 00:51:51.710
You will be back on this podcast.
你还会回到这个播客来的。

00:51:51.710 --> 00:51:54.389
You wrote so many frickin' all-timers.
你写了那么多绝妙的经典剧集。

00:51:54.389 --> 00:51:57.670
Like every episode you wrote was great.
就像你写的每一集都很棒。

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:08.510
I would say as an actor, when I would open that Manila envelope with, you know, I think on a Friday, they'd often have the Monday's table read episode waiting for you.
作为演员，当我打开那个马尼拉纸信封时——你知道的，我想通常是在周五，他们会把周一围读剧本的剧集放在那里等候着。

00:52:08.510 --> 00:52:13.510
And when I opened it up and I saw written by Chris Harris, I was like, all right, this is gonna be good.
当我打开它，看到上面写着“克里斯·哈里斯编剧”时，我就会想，好吧，这集肯定错不了。

00:52:13.510 --> 00:52:14.789
I'm excited about this.
我对这一集充满期待。

00:52:14.789 --> 00:52:18.349
There was always something in there that could only come from Chris.
那里总有些东西是只有克里斯才能写出来的。

00:52:18.349 --> 00:52:20.750
Many things in there that could only come from Chris.
有许多内容是只有克里斯才能创作的。

00:52:20.750 --> 00:52:38.630
And I think that is just such a testament That's the dream, I will say, as a showrunner of a TV show, that's the dream where somebody that can understand what the show is and bring the tone emotionally, bring the storytelling chops, but then do something with it that you yourself could never do and like Chris, like you just so have that talent.
我认为这真是一个绝佳的证明。作为一部电视剧的运作人，我的梦想就是这样——能够有人理解这部剧是什么，把握住情感基调，具备讲故事的能力，然后用它创作出连你自己都永远无法企及的东西。克里斯，你真的太有这种天赋了。

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:49.670
It's just kind of innate talent of bringing jokes only you could write and moments only you could create and to all of your work, including your amazing children's books and poetry books.
这就像是一种与生俱来的天赋，能把只有你能写出的笑话、只有你能创造的时刻带入到你所有的作品中，包括你精彩的儿童书和诗集。

00:52:49.670 --> 00:52:52.110
So go look those up, Chris is a genius at that too.
所以快去看看吧，克里斯在这方面也是个天才。

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:53.059
these final books.
这些最后的书。

00:52:53.059 --> 00:52:55.906
Playing James Bond and the hot things like it's great.
扮演詹姆斯·邦德以及那些热门角色，太棒了。

00:52:55.906 --> 00:52:56.750
They're amazing.
她们太棒了。

00:52:56.750 --> 00:52:57.906
She's extraordinary.
她太杰出了。

00:52:57.906 --> 00:52:58.947
That's what it is.
事实就是这样。

00:52:58.947 --> 00:53:00.277
You're amazing in that.
你在里面的表现太棒了。

00:53:00.277 --> 00:53:01.549
I love you in Fleabag.
我在《伦敦生活》里太喜欢你了。

00:53:05.230 --> 00:53:08.702
We love you Chris Harris and you will be back.
我们爱你，克里斯·哈里斯，你还会回来的。

00:53:08.702 --> 00:53:13.307
I love you guys can't wait and yeah you built the playground.
我爱你们，迫不及待了，没错，你们搭建了这座游乐场。

00:53:13.307 --> 00:53:16.100
It was so much fun to play around it.
在这里面玩耍太有趣了。

00:53:16.100 --> 00:53:33.236
So we tend to open these episodes with audio that you guys have submitted about how I met your mother means to you and we tend to close the show with written letters we've received about what how I met your mother means to you.
所以我们习惯用大家提交的关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你们意味着什么的音频来开启这些节目，我们也习惯用收到的关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你们意味着什么的信件来结束节目。

00:53:33.236 --> 00:53:44.485
If you would like to submit either an audio or a written letter, please go to how we made your mother.com, go to contact, should be clear from there.
如果你想提交音频或信件，请访问 how I made your mother.com，点击“联系我们”，从那里看应该很清楚。

00:53:44.485 --> 00:53:52.939
We have a wonderful letter today, and I often read these letters, but today I'm handing it over to Chris Harris.
我们今天有一封很棒的信，我经常读这些信，但今天我把它交给了克里斯·哈里斯。

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:55.739
Chris will you read us our closing letter?
克里斯，你能为我们读一下结尾的信吗？

00:53:55.739 --> 00:53:56.739
Your first mistake.
你的第一个错误。

00:53:56.739 --> 00:53:58.539
Here we go.
我们开始吧。

00:53:58.539 --> 00:54:07.139
Dear Josh and Craig, I was 12 years old the first time I watched How I Met Your Mother, which admittedly may have been a little too young for some of the more adult themes.
亲爱的乔希和克雷格，我第一次看《老爸老妈浪漫史》时只有12岁，诚实地说，对于其中一些偏向成人的主题来说，我当时可能有点太小了。

00:54:07.139 --> 00:54:08.139
We've been over this.
我们已经讨论过这个了。

00:54:08.139 --> 00:54:09.139
Yeah.
是的。

00:54:09.139 --> 00:54:11.099
This keeps coming up today.
这今天一直被拿出来说。

00:54:11.099 --> 00:54:18.420
But I had overheard my cousin talking about how great the show was, and I convinced my mom to get a Netflix subscription just so I could see it for myself.
但我无意中听到表哥谈论这部剧有多棒，于是我说服妈妈订购了Netflix会员，只为了能亲自看一看。

00:54:18.420 --> 00:54:22.980
At the time, I was incredibly lonely and already experiencing my first round of depression.
当时，我感到无比孤独，并且已经经历了第一轮抑郁。

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:28.699
I didn't have the words for it back then, but I knew that coming home and putting on how I met your mother felt like a lifeline.
那时候我还不懂用词来形容它，但我知道回到家打开《老爸老妈浪漫史》感觉就像是一根救命稻草。

00:54:28.699 --> 00:54:30.980
It gave me a group of friends I could visit every day.
它给了我一群每天都可以去拜访的朋友。

00:54:30.980 --> 00:54:33.059
It made me laugh when nothing else could.
在别无他法的时候，它让我笑了出来。

00:54:33.059 --> 00:54:37.019
It gave me hope that things could get better even when they felt impossible.
即使在感觉不可能的时候，它也给了我事情会变好的希望。

00:54:37.019 --> 00:54:39.099
The show has become this rhythm in my life.
这部剧已经成为我生活中的一种节奏。

00:54:39.099 --> 00:54:42.860
As soon as I finish the final episode, I go right back to the pilot episode.
我刚看完最后一集，就会立刻回到试播集重头再看。

00:54:42.860 --> 00:54:44.757
comfort, it's home, it's safe.
这是安慰，是家，是安全感。

00:54:44.757 --> 00:54:51.650
A while ago, I got a tattoo that says, right time, right place, from Ted's speech in season four, episode 22.
不久前，我纹了一个纹身，上面写着“对的时间，对的地点”，出自第四季第22集中泰德的演讲。

00:54:52.289 --> 00:54:56.530
When I finally went to New York, I made my friends come with me to McGee's Pub and I cried.
当我终于去到纽约时，我拉着朋友和我一起去了麦吉酒吧，我哭了。

00:54:56.530 --> 00:55:06.697
Not in a touristy, I'm checking this off my bucket list kind of way, but because stepping into that bar felt like stepping into a place that had held me through some of the darkest and brightest moments of my life.
不是那种游客式的、打卡愿望清单式的哭，而是因为走进那间酒吧，感觉就像是走进了一个在我生命中最黑暗和最明亮的时刻陪伴过我的地方。

00:55:06.697 --> 00:55:08.929
My heart felt full and strange and overwhelmed.
我的内心感到充实、奇妙而又难以言喻的激动。

00:55:08.929 --> 00:55:12.050
Like I was visiting an old friend, I'd only ever known through a screen.
就像我去探望了一位老朋友，一个我只通过屏幕认识的老朋友。

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:13.316
I could have stayed there forever.
我本可以在那里待上一辈子。

00:55:13.316 --> 00:55:14.210
I did not want to leave.
我一点也不想离开。

00:55:15.010 --> 00:55:18.449
Listening to your podcast has brought that world to life in a whole new way.
听你们的播客以一种全新的方式让那个世界复活了。

00:55:18.449 --> 00:55:23.995
It's filled me with so much joy and nostalgia, and one of the letters you read moved me more than I expected.
它让我充满了喜悦和怀旧之情，你们读过的一封信带给我的触动比我预期的还要深。

00:55:23.995 --> 00:55:32.186
It touched on things I've felt too, and for a moment I worried that writing to you would seem repetitive, but I've come to realize that's the magic of this show.
它触及了我也曾感受过的那些情绪，有一会儿我还担心写信给你们会显得有些重复，但我后来意识到，这就是这部剧的魔力所在。

00:55:32.186 --> 00:55:38.749
It created something so personal for so many of us, something that still connects people across time, distance, and circumstance.
它为我们许多人创造了如此私人化的东西，一种跨越时间、距离和环境依然能将人们联系在一起的东西。

00:55:38.749 --> 00:55:39.410
So thank you.
所以谢谢你们。

00:55:39.410 --> 00:55:50.603
Thank you for creating a show that's felt like home to me for over a decade, for giving us a group of characters who felt like friends, each of them flawed and funny and deeply human in ways that made me feel less alone.
谢谢你们创作了一部十多年来一直让我感到像家一样的剧集，谢谢你们赋予了我们一群像朋友一样的角色，他们每个人都有缺点、有趣且具有深深的人性，让我觉得自己不那么孤单了。

00:55:50.603 --> 00:55:52.130
And thank you for the podcast.
还要谢谢这个播客。

00:55:52.130 --> 00:55:58.734
Hearing your stories, reflections, and laughter has felt like revisiting something sacred with the people who made it.
倾听你们的故事、感想和笑声，感觉就像是和那些创造它的人一起重温了某种神圣的事物。

00:55:58.734 --> 00:56:02.315
It's been emotional, funny, and healing in ways I didn't expect.
它带来的情感共鸣、幽默和治愈超出了我的预期。

00:56:02.315 --> 00:56:04.330
How I met your mother has shaped me.
《老爸老妈的浪漫史》塑造了我。

00:56:04.330 --> 00:56:12.893
It's helped me hold on when things felt heavy and reminded me to believe in love, timing, and the kind of friendship that makes everything else bearable.
每当生活沉重时，它帮我挺了过来，并提醒我去相信爱情、时机，以及那种让一切变得可以忍受的深厚友谊。

00:56:12.893 --> 00:56:16.251
Thank you from the bottom of my heart for this amazing show.
我发自内心地感谢这部精彩的剧集。

00:56:16.251 --> 00:56:18.546
Sincerely, Louie from The Farrow Islands.
真诚的，来自法罗群岛的路易。

00:56:18.546 --> 00:56:19.046
Wow.
哇。

00:56:18.769 --> 00:56:19.643
And nice is that.
这太棒了。

00:56:19.643 --> 00:56:20.311
That's Chris.
那是克里斯。

00:56:20.311 --> 00:56:21.134
Isn't that cool?
是不是很酷？

00:56:21.134 --> 00:56:27.199
We've heard from fans and it's such an honor that we all got to make something that meant as much to people like that.
我们收到了粉丝的来信，大家能做出对人们有如此意义的作品，真的是我们的荣幸。

00:56:27.199 --> 00:56:27.969
That's amazing.
太神奇了。

00:56:27.969 --> 00:56:35.289
And so humbling to, yeah, thank you for sharing that.
而且令人感到谦卑，是的，谢谢你分享这些。

00:56:35.289 --> 00:56:36.289
It's beautiful.
太美好了。

00:56:36.289 --> 00:56:41.690
And to point this out, the feeling of friendship in the show, we all really were friends.
顺便指出这一点，剧集里那种友谊的感觉，我们真的都是朋友。

00:56:41.690 --> 00:56:46.489
I think the idea, the writing staff and the actors, we're all kind of similar ages and similar vibes.
我觉得编剧团队和演员们的想法，大家的年纪和气场都差不多。

00:56:46.489 --> 00:56:47.489
We all really were friends.
我们真的都是朋友。

00:56:47.489 --> 00:56:51.530
And Chris Harris, just to say this, and you can only say Chris Harris' name, you've got to say both names.
还有克里斯·哈里斯，顺便说一句，你只能连名带姓地叫克里斯·哈里斯。

00:56:51.530 --> 00:56:55.889
You can't just say Chris Harris, you have to say Chris Harris, he's one of those guys.
你不能只叫克里斯·哈里斯，你得说克里斯·哈里斯，他是那种人。

00:56:55.889 --> 00:56:58.610
that Chris is such a dear friend of mine and of Carter's.
克里斯是我和卡特非常亲密的朋友。

00:56:58.610 --> 00:57:00.369
Like we really do go back in our 20s.
我们从二十多岁就在一起了。

00:57:00.369 --> 00:57:02.050
We were writing for Late Show with David Letterman.
我们当时在为《大卫·莱特曼深夜秀》写剧本。

00:57:02.050 --> 00:57:13.210
We were going to McGee's pub on Thursday nights as a group right from the, that was the Late Show with David Letterman bar before it was the, now it's the how I met your mother bar, but it was the Late Show with David Letterman bar first.
星期四晚上我们会成群结队地去麦吉酒吧，那是《大卫·莱特曼深夜秀》的专属酒吧，后来才变成了《老爸老妈的浪漫史》酒吧，但一开始它是《大卫·莱特曼深夜秀》的酒吧。

00:57:13.210 --> 00:57:17.650
And we would go there, Chris, to sort of drink our troubles away after a long week.
克里斯，我们会在那里度过漫长的一周后，借酒浇愁。

00:57:17.650 --> 00:57:21.010
We'd do two shows, two tapings of Letterman on Thursday night.
我们在周四晚上要录制两场秀，两场莱特曼的节目。

00:57:21.010 --> 00:57:23.010
It was a great job, but it was very intense.
这是一份很棒的工作，但非常紧张。

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:24.489
And we would, we would be there.
然后我们就会在那里。

00:57:24.489 --> 00:57:35.489
And so Chris is like really such a dear friend of Carter and I, and came with us from the hair, and there was a little gap time where we weren't working together, but we were together on a couple of other things prior to how much a mother.
所以克里斯真的是我和卡特非常亲密的朋友，从《老爸老妈的浪漫史》之前就一直跟着我们，中间有一小段时间我们没在一起工作，但在《老爸老妈的浪漫史》之前我们一起做过其他几件事。

00:57:35.489 --> 00:57:38.010
Most of my TV career was with you, Chris.
我的大部分电视职业生涯都是和你一起度过的，克里斯。

00:57:38.010 --> 00:57:45.769
Is that I think that friendship and history that we all had, and so many of us had on the writing staff, and with the cast, I think it does translate into the final product.
我认为我们所有人、编剧团队中的许多人以及演员们之间的这种友谊和历史，确实体现在了最终的产品中。

00:57:45.769 --> 00:57:49.369
And maybe that's what this person who wrote this amazing letter was feeling.
也许这就是写这封精彩信件的人所感受到的。

00:57:49.369 --> 00:57:50.929
I like to think that's part of it.
我愿意认为这是其中的一部分。

00:57:50.929 --> 00:57:51.769
Absolutely.
绝对是的。

00:57:51.769 --> 00:57:58.070
It felt like for a while, it felt like how much a mother, Like, our lives were just a few years ahead of the characters.
有一段时间，感觉就像《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，我们的生活比角色们仅仅领先几年。

00:57:58.070 --> 00:58:04.449
And so just enough to let us reflect, but all of those times, I think you're right.
刚好够让我们反思，但所有那些时光，我想你是对的。

00:58:04.449 --> 00:58:08.570
I think that friendship and that genuine caring for each other really came through.
我觉得那种友谊和对彼此真诚的关怀真的流露出来了。

00:58:08.570 --> 00:58:24.829
Just to sort of emphasize how connected we were as both, you know, we were writers on this show, but also friends, I, when I was scrolling through the notes for doing game night.
为了强调我们之间的联系有多紧密，你知道，我们既是这部剧的编剧，也是朋友，当我翻看做游戏之夜的笔记时。

00:58:26.750 --> 00:58:29.269
Is that game going to find out secrets about each other or the rules are variable?
那个游戏会发现彼此的秘密吗？规则是变动的吗？

00:58:29.269 --> 00:58:30.110
They keep changing.
它们一直在变。

00:58:30.110 --> 00:58:34.590
And then it reads chain reaction reveal of dirt on other characters.
然后上面写着连锁反应，揭露其他角色的黑料。

00:58:34.590 --> 00:58:36.309
Barney's got something on Rob and Lily on Barney.
巴尼抓住了罗宾的把柄，莉莉抓住了巴尼的。

00:58:36.309 --> 00:58:39.710
And then the next line, it says, Chris Harris boy names.
接着下一行写着，克里斯·哈里斯男孩名字。

00:58:39.710 --> 00:58:55.070
And what is clearly happening here is my wife was pregnant and the room had paused to pitch on names for, for my child, it goes, Chris Harris boy names.
这里显然发生的事情是，我妻子当时怀孕了，房间里暂停下来为我的孩子想名字，上面写着，克里斯·哈里斯男孩名字。

00:58:55.150 --> 00:59:04.389
Casper, which, Casper, Jughead, Woody Woodpecker, SpongeBob, King Awesome, Chris Harris is- You went with King Awesome, you went with King Awesome.
卡斯帕，卡斯帕，大头呆，伍迪啄木鸟，海绵宝宝，超级无敌国王，克里斯·哈里斯是——你选了超级无敌国王，你选了超级无敌国王。

00:59:04.389 --> 00:59:05.230
You did awesome.
你选得太棒了。

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:06.070
You went with King Awesome and never looked back.
你选了超级无敌国王，而且再也没有回头。

00:59:06.070 --> 00:59:09.550
Chris Harris, girl names, Helga Sudoku Betty.
克里斯·哈里斯，女孩名字，海尔加 数独 贝蒂。

00:59:09.550 --> 00:59:13.710
That's how the notes page ends.
那页笔记就是这样结束的。

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:15.190
That's how the notes for that day end.
那天的笔记就是这样结束的。

00:59:15.190 --> 00:59:22.389
I also like, it's like an incredibly tender moment and tender ask, like, what's my friends?
我也很喜欢，这就像是一个极其温柔的时刻和温柔的请求，就像，我的朋友们怎么想？

00:59:22.389 --> 00:59:23.309
What did they think?
他们会想些什么？

00:59:23.309 --> 00:59:25.309
They're like, jughead, college jughead.
他们就像是，大头呆，大学大头呆。

00:59:25.309 --> 00:59:31.190
I am guilty, please equip me.
我有罪，请武装我。

00:59:31.190 --> 00:59:35.699
All sins are forgiven, New York City.
一切罪孽皆被赦免，纽约市。

